Id: boot011w
Severity: WARN
Category: boot
Check: check_secureboot
References: UEFI specification, chapter 32 (Secure Boot and Driver Signing)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST CM-14, SI-7(9); ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

The firmware is in Secure Boot setup mode: no platform key (PK) is
enrolled. In this mode the key databases are not protected, so any
program that runs, including one started from a USB stick, can enrol
keys of its own, and the firmware verifies nothing while it lasts.

To fix it, restore the factory keys in the firmware setup (often
"Restore Factory Keys" or "Install default Secure Boot keys") and turn
Secure Boot on, or enrol your own platform key if you manage your own
keys (sbctl, for instance, does this).
